In files that expose a shared API, e.g., chunix/challoc.c and
chunix/chaos.c we could do:

#if BSD41
Original code inlined
#elif SOCKET
#include "challoc-socket.c"
#else
#include "challoc-embed.c"
#endif

Suggested options:

	BSD41
	LINUX / LINUX41200
	OPENBSD
	SOCKET
	EMBED

But it probobly makes more sense to split up chunix/ into:

  chsocket/challoc.c
  chlinux/challoc.c
  chunix/challoc.c
  chlocal/challoc.c

etc, than using #if hackery.  Then having a CHBKEND variable that can
be changed, which is used in $(CHBKEND)/challoc.c in the Makefile to
specify the source files to be used.

Chaosnet for Unix -- (Suggested New) Directory Layout

include/chaos/*.h
	These are the header files needed by the kernel and user
	programs that deal with the Chaos network.

libhosts/* and libhosts/hosttab.h
	These files create a library of host table manipulation
	routines and actually build and install new host tables.  The
	source file from which the actual host table is built also
	resides here.  This also inclueds the the header file for the
	host table library.  See the README file in this directory.

cmd/*
	This directory contains all sources for user programs, and the
	make file to compile and install them all.  It also contains
	this file.

chncp/*
	This directory contains sources for the part of the kernel
	code that is independent of the operating system it is run on
	(largely).

chunix/*
	This directory contains the UNIX interface to the network,
	which implements the network software as a UNIX device driver.

chlinux/*
	This direcory contains the Linux interface to the network,
	which implements the network software as a Linux kernel
	module.

chsocket/*
	This directory contains a socket interface to the network,
	which implements the network software as a daemon.

chembed/*
	This directory contains a embeddeable interface to the
	network.

OpenBSD kernel driver

	Copy source tree to /sys/netchaos.  Modify required
	autoconf(9) files.  The cmd/ commands then uses ioctl(2) to
	communicate (wrapped around a agnostic chopen.c)

Linux kernel driver

	Point out kernel in Kbuild, and run `make' in chlinux/
	directory.  The cmd/ commands then uses ioctl(2) to
	communicate (wrapped around a agnostic chopen.c)

Socket driver

	Run make in cmd/, and start `chaosd'.  The cmd/ commands then
	write/read from a socket (wrapped around a agnostic chopen.c)

Embedded

	Compile libchembed.a (doesn't exist yet); then link whatever
	program against it.

Notes on linage

Original in this context means that the files haven't been modified by
someone other than the original authors.

chaos-19820812
	Original and complete tape; from DLW.

v8
	Contains kernel code (chncp/chunix), but no no user-space
	code.  While the tree is dated 1985, but the code under
	usr/sys/{chncp,chunix} is rather from between 1982 and 1984.

	E.g., chstream.c was modified such that it would accept an
	errorp argument between 1982 and 1984.  This change does not
	exist in the above V8 code.  It also does not contain RCS
	headers.  Plus, the directory structure for the headers is the
	old split-style (e.g, chaos.h does not contain the literal
	content of constants.h but instead does an #include).

chaos-19870606
	Incomplete dump of kernel code (chncp/chunix), no user-space
	code.

	I think this tree should rather be dated 1990-07-17,
	originally based on a tree from 1987 (1987-06-09).  The 1994
	timestamps are a rouse.  When CFIELDS in 1994 setup
	/afs/dev.mit.edu/source/src77/bsd-4.3/vax/sys/attic/chncp he
	started numbering from 1.1 (when he re-ci:ed the files).

	The original location of this was once
	/source/bsd-4.3/vax/sys, where PROBE also did a re-ci of some
	files (1990-07-17), including some minor fixes for compiler
	issues.

	As to why I claim that this is specifically based on something
	from 1987, is simply that RC did actual changes then
	(1987-06-12).  The initial copy of all the files is though
	from 1984.  Which is I guess when Symbolics forked the
	Chaosnet for Unix code, which is around there I think.

chaos-19970521
	Contains many original files (~1989) as backup files, or
	unmodified.  Also contains a kernel driver for Linux 2.0.18.
	Brad probobly started working on this in 1996?

chaos-19991124-0
	Contains RCS files but these are only one revision and not
	original.  Probobly a dump of the CVSROOT.  The Linux driver
	is for 2.0.18 as above.

chaos-19991124-1
	Same as chaos-19991124 but a working copy.

chaos-20000703
	Continuation of above? Seems to be exactly same, but with RCS
	IDs updates by me.

chaos-20010506
	Continuation of above.  Minor changes to keep up with Linux
	breakage.  The Linux driver is for 2.2.18 (or 2.4.2?).

chaos-20180318
	Contains the start of a semi-cleaned up version of Chaosnet
	for Unix that might work with three different back ends
	(kernel for OpenBSD and Linux, Unix socket, embedded) and has
	the Linux driver updated for 4.11.3.
